{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Niraula2015-15layer-Si-SiO2-bandpass",
  "Source": "M. Niraula, J. W. Yoon, and R. Magnusson, \"Single-layer optical bandpass filter technology,\" Opt. Lett. 40(21), 5062-5065 (2015). doi:10.1364/OL.40.005062 - Fig. 1(b) caption; Fig. 1(c) dashed curve; text p. 5063",
  "Notes": "Classical multilayer narrowband bandpass filter at 1304 nm (14-layer quarter-wave Bragg stack with one embedded half-wave defect), used as a comparison to the single-layer grating filter\nSpecification: Narrow transmission peak at 1304 nm with flat low stop bands (T <= 1 %) over 1250-1350 nm; nearly identical to the single-layer grating filter (peak 99.8 %, FWHM 0.9 nm)\nReported: Fig. 1(c): multilayer T0 curve nearly coincides with the grating's: peak ~100 % at ~1304 nm, FWHM ~0.9 nm, T well below 1 % from 1250 to 1350 nm away from the peak (read from plot; the numbers 99.8 % and 0.9 nm are stated for the grating design).\nNotes: Caption lists 'd1 = 93.7 nm and d2 = 215.9 nm' as quarter-wave thicknesses and 'Layer 8 from the top has a half-wave thickness of 431.8 nm' (= 2 x d2, so SiO2). With a 14-layer Bragg stack plus defect in 15 layers and layer 8 SiO2, layers alternate Si/SiO2 starting with Si at the top (the inset of Fig. 1(b) shows n1 first, under the cover). Cover and substrate both n = 1.51 (nC = nS = 1.51 per caption, i.e. SiO2-clad). TE (s) polarization at normal incidence (irrelevant at 0 deg). Check: 3.48 x 93.7 = 326.1, 1.51 x 215.9 = 326.0 nm -> quarter-wave at 1304 nm. k of Si ~1e-6 neglected. Stop-band merit edges 1300/1308 nm are my choice to exclude the passband.\nLayers from Fig. 1(b) caption; Fig. 1(c) dashed curve; text p. 5063; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours peak T 100 % at 1304.12 nm, FWHM 1.17 nm; paper peak ~100 % at ~1304 nm, FWHM ~0.9 nm (stated for the grating).",
